WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:05.070
I recently created a database table for
a college called student table.

2
00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:10.594
It contains the following pieces of deed
on each student in the college ID, first

3
00:00:10.594 --> 00:00:16.541
name, last name, home address, college
address, contact number and department.

4
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.771
Let's use the update syntax to
update the home address and

5
00:00:20.771 --> 00:00:25.090
contact number of the student assigned
the ID of three in the table.

6
00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:30.393
So I click the SQL tab in phpMyAdmin.

7
00:00:30.393 --> 00:00:35.048
Now I use the update clause followed
by the name of the table that I want to

8
00:00:35.048 --> 00:00:37.156
update which is student table.

9
00:00:37.156 --> 00:00:42.457
Then I add the set clause followed by
the names of the columns to be updated,

10
00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:45.586
which our home address and contact number.

11
00:00:45.586 --> 00:00:49.616
Next to the name of each column,
I add an equal to symbol and

12
00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:55.860
place the new values to be inserted into
the table in single quotation marks.

13
00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:01.890
I also make sure to separate these
column value pairings with a comma.

14
00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.674
Finally, I add the WHERE cause,

15
00:01:04.674 --> 00:01:08.562
to identify the exact
record I want to update.

16
00:01:08.562 --> 00:01:12.077
This record has a student ID column field,

17
00:01:12.077 --> 00:01:18.690
that was assigned the value of three so
I write where ID equals three.

18
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:23.687
Now that I've completed the syntax,
I can select go.

19
00:01:23.687 --> 00:01:29.000
I then receive a message confirming
that the change has been made.

20
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.445
And when I check the table,
it displays the updated values for

21
00:01:32.445 --> 00:01:35.171
the assigned columns
alongside student three.

22
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.090
So that's how you update information for
one student.

23
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.720
However, the update
syntax can also be used

24
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.285
to update the information from
multiple students at once.

25
00:01:46.285 --> 00:01:50.608
Let's suppose that the college's
engineering department has moved their

26
00:01:50.608 --> 00:01:54.262
classes to a new location on
campus called the Harper Building.

27
00:01:54.262 --> 00:01:57.248
And I need to update the department's
address on the table for

28
00:01:57.248 --> 00:01:58.634
all engineering students.

29
00:01:58.634 --> 00:02:03.320
I can perform this task
using the update SQL syntax.

30
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.479
The syntax is very similar
to the previous example.

31
00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:13.497
First I used the update clause
followed by the name of the table.

32
00:02:14.862 --> 00:02:19.576
Then I add the set clause and
state that I want to update the values

33
00:02:19.576 --> 00:02:24.130
within the college address column,
to Harper building.

34
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:29.351
So I type this as SET college
address equals Harper Building.

35
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.883
Next, I type the WHERE clause and
state that I want this

36
00:02:38.883 --> 00:02:44.074
update to occur within
the college address column for

37
00:02:44.074 --> 00:02:51.361
all students assigned the value of
engineering in the department column.

38
00:02:52.624 --> 00:02:54.841
I then click go to run the statement.

39
00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:59.772
Now, I just check that the table
has updated the college address of

40
00:02:59.772 --> 00:03:03.479
the engineering department
to Harper Building.

41
00:03:03.479 --> 00:03:05.682
I could also use the update statement,

42
00:03:05.682 --> 00:03:08.701
to update multiple fuel
values in multiple records.

43
00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:14.552
For example, I can return to
the original SQL statement.

44
00:03:14.552 --> 00:03:19.980
And add a new column value
pairing to the set clause.

45
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.786
If I want to update the home
address column, I add a comma and

46
00:03:25.786 --> 00:03:32.771
write home address equals the new
address information within quotes.

47
00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:37.430
Make sure to separate out the column
value pairings with a comma.

48
00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.420
In this example I'm updating two
columns with one update statement.